t\n1184\tNoTE.-7Next we have\n1185\t\n1186\t\n1187\tTHE IMPRESSIVE REVIEW\n1188\t\n1189\t\n1190\tFiction has been so standardized that\n1191\tthe picturesque heterogeneousness Of\n1192\thalf century ago has been sadly di-\n1193\tminished. Against this abominable\n1194\tmodernized Standardization arises that\n1195\tpast master of satire and wit-Prof.\n1196\tstephen Leacock His most recent\n1197\twork. \"Winsome Winnie and Other\n1198\tNonsense Novels\" comes to us guised\n1199\tas humor, but even the undiscerning\n1200\twill detect that he has struck straight\n1201\tat the vitals of modern literature in\n1202\tall it's horrid decadence, using as\n1203\tweapons his sharp sword of ridicule\n1204\tand the blasting power of his un-\n1205\tmatched ability to score at the ex-\n1206\tpense of his contemporaries without\n1207\tstraining for effect.\n1208\t\n1209\t\n1210\tNorE.-It betokens vast knowl-\n1211\t\n1212\t\n1213\tedge on the part of a reviewer to\n1214\t\n1215\t\n1216\tprove that some one who wrote\n1217\t\n1218\t\n1219\tbook never wrote it at all. Recall\n1220\t\n1221\t\n1222\tIng the Shakespeare-Bacon contro-\n1223\t\n1224\t\n1225\tversy and others we now put the\n1226\t\n1227\t\n1228\tCorona on the baby's lap and write\n1229\tTHE WISE GUY REVIEW:\n1230\t\n1231\t\n1232\tWhile Stephen Leacock's name is\n1233\tattached to the new volume of satirical\n1234\tnovelettes under the title Of Win\n1235\tsome Winnie\" it has come to us that\n1236\tthe real author of the work is none\n1237\tother than Mark Twain. This belief in\n1238\tliterary circles has gained credence\n1239\tever since 1t became known that Prof.\n1240\tLeacock was seen experimenting with\n1241\ta ouija board at a Toronto tea\n1242\t\n1243\t\n1244\t1t has been pointed out that Lea\n1245\tcock is not the kind of a man who\n1246\twould waste time reading seasons\n1247\tcrop of love stories, civil war tales. de-\n1248\ttective yarns, political novels, narra-\n1249\ttives of the sea and other products of\n1250\tleading literary lights. And if he never\n1251\tread them it would manifestly be i'm\n1252\tpossible for him to have outranked the\n1253\trankest popular sellers.\n1254\t\n1255\t\n1256\tOn the other hand. Mark Twain has\n1257\thad virtually nothing to do since his\n1258\tdeath. The savants, consequently, hold\n1259\tthe theory that Mark Twain did the\n1260\twork, while Prof. Leacock with the\n1261\taid OF d ouija board gets the credit.\n1262\tA very simple premise. is it not? Yes,\n1263\tvery simple.\n1264\t\n1265\t\n1266\tNoTE.-Our wife, who is serious\n1267\t\n1268\t\n1269\tminded, has read over our shoulder\n1270\t\n1271\t\n1272\tto here. Now she suggests that we\n1273\t\n1274\t\n1275\ttry to write\n1276\t\n1277\t\n1278\tA HUMOROUS REVIEW.\n1279\t\n1280\t\n1281\tWe can't!\n1282\t\n1283\t=== 26_1843-07-14_pna_sn84023127_00200290197_1843071401_0541.json\n1284\tBurlington free press. [volume] (Burlington, Vt.) 1827-1865 · 1843-07-14\n1285\tcrops: None\n1286\theadline: \n1287\t---\n1288\tremember, and remember only, the lost hus-\n1289\tband of my spirit-world. perform, coldly\n1290\tand scornfully, the unheavenly necessities of\n1291\tthis temporary existence and from the WIN\n1292\tdows of my prison (black-like the glimpses\n1293\tof the midnight heaven they let in) look out\n1294\tfor the coming Of my spirit-lord. Lonely !!\n1295\tlonely\n1296\t\n1297\t\n1298\tThou wouldst know, perhaps, what SEM\n1299\tblance l bear since my mortal separation\n1300\tfrom thee. Alas the rose, not the lily,\n1301\treIgns upon my cheek would not dis\n1302\tappoint thee though of tina, there is little\n1303\tfear, for thou lowest for the spirit only. But\n1304\tbelieve not, because health holds me rudely\n1305\tdown, and seem not fragile and ready to\n1306\tdepart; believe not, oh bridegroom of my\n1307\tsoul that bear willingly my neshy fetter,\n1308\tor endure with patience the degrading hom-\n1309\tage to its beauty. For there are soulless\n1310\tworms who think me fair. Ay,i t