du lsssssarhusetts,a prononce, a propos du Kansas\ndiscotss dune violence inouie ct qui depasse en-\nvlrulence les harangues les plus revoltantes que\nnous ayons lanlais iues.\n\n\n! M. Snmne1 est Embarrassment I'm homme de\n! talent ct il en- deploye bea ucoup dams son dis\n| cours. II ren est qye plus coupable nos yeux\ncar ceux-la ont de grands devoirsa rempr qui pos\nsedent lie precieux don de a parole. LIE senateur\ndu Massachusetts, cherchG ) ST. rmatsefle Sud ct\nttIi imprimer une honteuse fetrissple. Non con-\ntent de dire que fesclavage etat lie crie des crss,\nil sgnae tous les Etats on existe cetta institution\nCONNIE de wills repaires de brigands.\nCes generalites quoique blessantes, eussent\npeuetre etc tolerees, nlais I'orateur prsa tache\nd'insnlter successivement dams son maencontreux\ndiscours toutes les notabiIites dn Senat Cass.\nDouglass Mason. etc, lui ont re pondu les uns du\nton du plus profond mepris, les autres avec une\nhauteur ecrasante. NJ. Butler, de la Caroline du\nSud, etait seul absent ct cest ustement lui que NJ\nSumner await lie plus matraite.\n\n\nA rissue de la seance, M. Brooks, representant\nde Ia Caroline du Sud ct parent de NJ. Butler en-\nira dams l'eneeinte da Senat ct adressant de Vlfs\nrepToches M. Sumner, lie frappa rudetnent sur\nles epaues eft 4 la tate dune came en- caontchoac\nquill await la main. Scion une version, Ill. Sum-\nner. etonrdi par les premiers coups. serait tonbe\nsans connaissance sur lie parquet ct M BFoosss au-\nrait continue a lie frapper avec Ia meme violence\nsans que personne snnge&t ntervenr. Excel\nlente preure de peu de sympathie qu'insplra.t ia\nvictirne.\n\n\nQuoiquil en- soft, de pareilles scenes dams pinte-\nrieur meme du capitole sont deplorables. Nous\nconprencns que le discours de NJ Summer ait re-\n\n\n\n\n\ndu, mais nous ne concevons pas qn'on~i'ait chatie\ndams la sale des deliberations du Senat NJ Brooks\naurait do attendre saa moins que lie senateur de\nMassachusetts f1t hors du capitoe. ct meme alors\nles voies de fat don't il s'est rendu conpabe sont\ninexcusables II est descendu presque aussi bas\nque NJ. Sumner. 81 ceini-ci etc violent or pa\nroles son adversaire etc encore plus violent en-\naction NJ Brooks ma venge persenne ct SIM\nplement eleve un piedestai III. Sumner que les\nabolitionstes du Nord wont mansenant poser EN-\nmartyr de la bonne cause. 81 an lien de repondfe\nEngagement aux saletes ct aux impertneDces du\nsenatenr dn Massac husetts fut borne lie trail\nter avec lie m&pris quill merite ct pacabler sous\nquelques-nnes de ces paroles qui tuent plus SIRE\nment qu'un discours, lie chatiment cut etc plus\ncomplet car finsulteur cut etc hamilie.\n\n\nNJ. Sumner, on lie savait, ne lutte quavec la pa\nrole; il a pris Engagement, bien quill ne nlanque\npoint de courage, de ne annals Fepondre aux pro-\nvocations de fait, de ne jamaIs accepter de defs.\n\n\n\n\n\nles luttes physiques qui les evitent ct qu ament\nmieux reeevoir dix coups que den rendre TN. En-\nlie frappant, d. Brooks n'ignorait pas quill ne sex\nposait ancun danger. Un seaateur du Sud on de\nFOaest br0le la cervelle au collegue gu ose Pas\nsaillir ou lui plonge couteau dams sens Cette\nfacon de Sc deendre peut paraftre quelque peu bru\ntale, mais ellie est dams nos m9urs. Puisqae NJ.\nSummer ne pouvalt pas employer ce noyen de de-\ntense, pourquoi pa-t-onfrappe2\n\n\nMais, nous dira-t-on, serait-il done perrais un\ninsulteur de letter impunement pnjure d Ia face\ndun honnete homme parce quill pris l'engage-\nment de ne pas Tspondre aux voes de faita Eth!\nmon Dieu, serez-vous beaueoup plus avance lors-\nque vous u aurez adrnnstre des c,aips de came\nct tpempacherez-vous de vons injurier nne seconde\nfois2 Non, certes. NJ. Sumner traitera probable\nment fort NAL NJ Brooks dams son proclaim dis\ncours, ct la crainte dun nouveau scandale empa-\nchera M. Brooks de lui administers nne seconde\nfiageiIation. Ii ~ asdeschoses auxquelles il faut\nbien Sc resigner La boue ct les immondices sont\ndams Ia nature ct il est impossible de les eviter.\n\n\n.1beiIe1\n\n=== 12_1961-06-13_p2_sn83045462_00280608944_1961061301_1076.json\nEvening star. · 1961-06-13\ncrops: None\nheadline: Bishops Number Up,\nIt's ST in Sing Sing\n---\nNEW YORK, June 13 (AP)\nThere was something ode\nabout the way the bishop\" oi\nBrooklyn's Ideal Spiritualistic\nIndependent Church of Faith\nOf America handled his con-\ngregation.\n\n\nFor instance. he would lie In\na coffin murmnring incanta-\ntionS as his flock filed by and\ndropped SIS and $40 bills over\nhis purple velvet robes.\n\n\nOccasionally, investigators\nsaid, he would mix in a likely\npolicy number with his in\ncarnations.\n\n\nThe Brooklyn District attor-\nney's office, at the request of\nthe National Association for the\nAdvancement of Colored Peo-\n\n=== 6_1904-06-25_p1_sn84023296_00200296151_1904062501_0841.json\nSpirit of the age. [volume] (Woodstock, Vt.) 1845-1913 · 1904-06-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: THE TIME TO SELECT YOUR VA.\n\n\nCATION HAUNT.\n\nMountains, Seashore and Lake Re-\n\n\nsorts.\n---\nsorts.\n\n\nThe trees and foliage are decked in\ntheir summer garb, and the perfumed\nblossoms are beginning TO fall: the\nsuns rays are hotter than a few weeks\nago; the ripening and fresh color of\nspring has deeDened into the mellow\ncoat of summer. In another week.\nperspiring business men will be rush\ning pell nell for the nearest cooling\nspot. The beaches will be thronged;\nand the country and mountain resorts\nwill be preparing for the rush. You\nare not obliged to swelter under the\nsummers sun. nor to tread the baked\nbricks of the city, ether-at least not\non your vacation, and it behooves you\nto take note of the numerous and va\nried resort in northern New England\nand make selection. Do you like the\ncountry and a modern hotel? Do you\nprefer quiet, old farmhouse with pic.\ntoria1 surroundings? Do you care to\npaddle in the ocean or to sun yourself\non the sandy beach? Do you desire to\nHE to mountain top, to drink in the\npure atmosphere of these heights? Do\nyou wish to recline in a peaceful val.\nley of absorbing scenery, where sill\nvery streams and verdant plains make\nup vision of pastoral beauty?\n\n\nYou can choose your resort. any\nthing you want in the Boston e\nNames list of resorts, with hotels and\nrates for 1904. This booklet is some\nthing entirely new 1t contains\nbeautiful colored cover and ninety-six\npages of interesting descriptive mat\nter information and illustrations. 1t\nwill be sent free upon receipt of ad-\ndress by the general passenger depart\nment. B. G. VI. R. R., Boston.\n\n=== 49_1895-05-25_p1_sn88056017_00415666718_1895052501_0995.json\nBlackfoot news. (Blackfoot, Idaho) 1891-1902 · 1895-05-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: THE PHANTOM\n---\non MAY TALK\nabout your ortho-\ndox ghost. who\nhaunt ancient cas-\nties, wailing and\ngrooming. and car\nrying flaming lights\nfrom window to\nwindow for appar-\nemily no earthly, or\nrather unearthly,\npurpose save that\nOf fat.t,ifyipg ont Of\n\n=== 4_1890-08-11_pna_sn90059522_00175033506_1890081101_0389.json\nSt. Paul daily globe. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn.) 1884-1896 · 1890-08-11\ncrops: None\nheadline: SARATOGA'S OFFERING.\n\nFive Good Races on the Gard for\n\n\nTODAY.\n---\nSARATOGA, N. Y. Aug. 10.-Five\nraces, three of them of the short dis\nlance of five furIongs, are scheduled for\nMonday.\n\n\nFirst race, five furlongs- Rainbow, 90:\nDaly-Gypsy 1illy, 90; Dahlia los; Ballyhoo,\n9S Bertelle, 90; Genevieve 100; Keystone,\nno; Ben McCree, 9S; Ofalce los; Carnot,\nITO; Radcliffe, lu3\n\n\nSecond race five furlongs-Princess Annie,\n1UI: Ed Hopper, lO2; Hypocrite, H.; Lady\nlog; King Crab, 10.\n\n\nThird race, two-year-olds, five furlongs-\nAvalon, 10; Planchette, 10o; Jeanerette,\n10Y; Pokeno, 100; Little Bess, lr2: Lee S,\nno; Margherita, 110 Maud B, 102: Ma belle,\n100 Pluto, lO5; Allan Bane, 10; Bothera-\nHon-TWilight colt. I05.\n\n\nFourth race, three-y ear-olds. mile-Fox-\nmode, los; Mamie p, 90; Little Crete, IS;\nSena, SO; Kosciusko Kay, 9S; Mora. 9S;\nPrincess Limo. 1903; Corticelli, 80; Marie X,\nJOB; Worth, 1o8; Carter B, l0s; Jack Rose,\nlib.\n\n\nFifth race, selling, mile and seventy yards\nBirthday, IS; Harry Weldon, 100; Sallie\no, 106; Barrister ll3; Pullman, f00 Rollin\nHaWley, 10Y.\n\n\nTHE FOOLS.\n\n\nFirst Race-Goiden ROD. $50; Ballyhoo,\nSIS; Gcnevievc, SD; Ramboro. Gypsy Filly,\nOfalece, Carnot and Radcliffe, S. each; 9th\ners. So EACH\n\n\nSecond Race-King Crab and Hypocri tie,\n$50 EACH Princess Annie. Lady Pulsifer and\nAustralia, SIS each; Field, So.\n\n\nThird Race-Allen Bane. $780, Ma Belle,\n$50; Plauchette and Maud Sc each; others\nSO each.\n\n\nFourth Race-Princess Limo, S25; Worth,\nBIS; FoXmeade, SD; Nora 10; Little Crete,\nMarie K, Corticelh Carter B and Jack Rose\n81 each; others S. each.\n\n\nFilth Race-No selling.\n\n\nThese may win: First race, Golden Rod\ntId S0T roce I hen Rane et and Ma\nBelle; fourth race, Worth ana Little Crete;\nfifth race, Barrister and Rollin Hawley.\n\n=== 6_1847-06-29_p1_sn84024082_0021247163A_1847062901_0189.json\nJuliet signal. [volume] (Juliet [i.e. Joliet], Ill.) 1844-1??? · 1847-06-29\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nblazing volcano in the midnight Heavens.\nNoiseiess and swift, the fearless patriots\nKept on their way, when !o as they came\nto the marsh, they saw only smooth\nsheet OF water--the tide was up flooding\nthe whole ground; the brave fellows\npaused moment. as this new and unex.\npected obstacle crossed their path, but at\nthe stern forward of their leaders. thew\n/ boldly plunged in, and without drum or\n! bugie note to cheer their steady courage.\nmoved on In dead silence straight on the\npalisades. The noise had now alarmed\nthe sentinels, and the rapid discharge of\ntheir muskets through the gloom was fol-\nlowed by lights moving swiftly about Ola\nthe ramparts, and hurried shouts of so\narms! TO Grasp and the fierce roll of\ndrums, rousing up the garrison from its\ndream of security. The next moment\nthat dark rock was one mass Of fame, as\nthe artillery and musketiy opened along\nits sides, shedding Iurid light on the\ncountenances of the men below and 'Ad.\nunnce! AdrGncep rung in startling accents\nalong the ranks.\n\n\n\"the ramparts were alive with SOL\ndiers, and amid shouts and hurried words\nOF command, the fiery torrents from the\nsummit kept on these devoted men. The\nwater around them was driven into spray\nby the grape shot and balls thai fell in an\nincessant shower, while the Kissing, bur\n\n\nsting shells traversing the air in every d.\n\n\nrectlon, added inconceivable terror 10 the\nscene. Yet these fororn hopes toiled\nvigorously on, and heaved away OF the\nGd6GttaS to open gap for the column,\nthat, without returning a shot, stood and\ncrumbIed under the fire, waiting with\nfixed bayonets to rush to the assault. At\nthe head of one of these was Wayne,\nchatting like lion in the tolls, at the OBJ\nstacies that arrested his progress. The\ntorlorn hope in front of him, worked\nsteadily Oh in the very blaze of the bat\nteries, and the rapid blows of th sir axes\nwere heard in the intervals ofthe thu der\nOF artillery, that shook the midnight air,\nwhile one after another dropped dead in\nhis footsteps, till out of the twenty that\nstarted, only three stood up unharmed.-\nYet still their axes fell steady and strong\nuntil an opening was made through\nwhich the columns could pass, and then\nthe shout of Wayne was heard above the\ndin and tuInult, summoning his followers\non. With fixed bayonets they marched\nsterniy through the portals made at such\nnoble sacrifice and pressed furiously for\nward--through the morass-over every\nobstacie-up TO the very mouths of the\ncannon, and up the rocky activity, they\nstormed on crushing everything in their\npassage. Towering al the head of his\nshattered column, pointing still onward\nand upward, with his glittering blade, and\nndiDg his thrilling shout back over his\nfollowers, Wayne strobe steadily up the\nheight, till at length struck in the head by\nmusket ball, he fell backward amid\nthe ranks. Instantly rising on one knee\nhe cried ost-'March on! carry me into\nthe fort, for will die at the head of my\ncolumn! And those heroes put their\nbrave arms around him and bore him on.\nNot shot was fired, but taking the rap\nid volleys on their unshrinking breasts,\ntheir bayonets glittering in the flash oil\nthe enemy,s guns. they kept on over the\nliving and the dead, shifting down the\nveteran ranks that threw themselves in\nvain valor before them, till they reached\nthe centre ofthe fort. where they met the\nother column, which, over the same OBJ.\nstacles, had achieved the same triumph\nAt the sight of each other, one loud shout\nshook the heights and rolled down the\nbleeding line-was again sent back till\nthe Heavens rung with the wild huzzas,\nand then the flag of freedom went up and\nHaunted away on the midnight air. The\nthick volumes of smoke that lay around |\nthat rock, slowly lifted and rolled up the\nHudson, the stars appeared once more in\nthe sky, and all was over. the lordly\nriver went sweeping by as it had done\nduring the deadly strife that cast such\nbaIeful light on its bosom, and darkness,\nand death. like silence. shrouded the\nshores. Mournfully and slow those for\nlorn- hopes and their brave companions\nwho had fallen in the assault, were bro't\nup from their CORY beds and conveyed to\nthe grave.\n\n\n\"Wayne's wound proved not to be se-\nvere-the ball only having grazed the\nskull for two inches. and he lived TO wear\nthe laurels grateful nation placed on his\nbrow. The country rung with his name\nand Congress presented him with gold\nmedal. The whole plan of the assault\nwas most skillfully laid, and the bearing\nOF Wayre, throughout was gallantin the\nextreme. He chose the post of danger\nat the head of his column. and led his men\nwhere even the bravest might shrink to\nfollow, and when struck and apparently\ndying, heroically demanded to be carried\nforward, that he might die in the arms of\nvictory, or be left where the last stand\nwas made. His troops were worthy Of\nsuch leader, and more gallant officers\n\n=== 27_1897-06-26_pna_sn78000395_0021247969A_1897062601_0027.json\nThe labor world. [volume] (Duluth, Minn.) 1896-current · 1897-06-26\ncrops: None\nheadline: PUMpHOUSE SPECTER\n\nCised. Who\n\n\n,This Time?\n., Investigate.\n\nlater Works Purchase Question\nComes Up.-Extra Session.\n\n\nMcGiIvray's Bond.\n---\nnight started out insufferably\ndull but wound up with an en-\npertaining burst OF excitement.\nAldermen Hansen and Shannon\nwere absent, and as neither side\nwas able to muster on the I'M\nportant issues q majority of the\nentire membership OF the council\nlittle progress was made.\n\n\nThe mayor submitted the APP\npointments of Christian Johnson\nas spokesman at West Duluth and\nHerman Vending to be pound\nmaster at Woodland. The\nformer after a lengthy discussion\nwas turned down, the latter re-\nferred, and the former appoint\nment of D. D. Mcdonald special\npoliceman without pay at the\ninstance OF the Cranberry Lun-\nDer company was confirmed.\n\n\nThe fire commissioners sub\nn1itted a report of the salvage\nCORPS from the board OF fire\nUnderwriters. The report gives\na list of the stockholders and a\nfinancial statement which shows\nthe total receipts up to June IS,\n1897, to be So, 538 .46, of which\nthere remains a balance OF\n$3,14O.S5, all of which will be\nneeded to keep up the service\nuntil the 1897 tax is due from\nthe insurance companies.\n\n\nThe city attorney reported that\nMr.. Patton would still be city\nengineer until his successors\nbond had been approved. Notice\nwas ordered given that the city\nwould no longer require Great\nEastern hall for armory pur-\nposes Estimates were allowed\nto the Carroll Porter Company\nfor $15,s5t:24 and to T. w.\nHugo for $93.92.\n\n\nThe board of public works,\nprotested against dispensing\nwith the superintendent of\nstreet sprinkling; the bond of\nFred Voss for 100000 was\napproved; the final estimate to\nG. J. Frederickson was held up; a\nstring of miscellaneous bills was\nallowed, during the roll call on\nwhich Alderman Jefferson ex-\nplanned that while, voting for the\nmotion to allow, he did not ap-\nprove OF the Hartman com\nmission allowance.\n\n\nA proposition to let a contract\nfor the steel work connected with\nthe pumphouse was tabled until\nthe new city engineer has quail\nflied; the uniform charter bill for\n$63.22 was allowed, after it had\nbeen explained that the bill\ncould not be collected by law,\nbut that the council had a right\nto pay it if it was deemed a just\ndebt.\n\n\nThe St. Paul & Duluth rail-\nroad was instructed to either\nkeep a Waterman rput in gates\nat the crossings Of 57th, 59th\nand 63rd avenues west over\ntheir tracks. And after much\ndiscussion a proposition to dis\nperse with one of the fire\nwardens was tabled.\n\n\nAt this point things began to\nwarm up and the audience grew\ninterested. Alderman Burg\nwanted a committee of two citi-\nzens from each ward to be ap-\npointed by the president to act\nin conjunction with the water\nworks committee relative to the\npurchase of the water plant.\nA motion to lay on the table\nfailed, the roll call standing as\nfollows:\n\n\nYeas-Dahl, Duren, Jefferson,\nSimpson, Stevens and Trevll-\nliOn.--6.\n\n\nNays - Burg, Crassweller,\nHarker, Richardson, Rowley,\nSang and Tischer.-7.\n\n\nPresident Cochrane, who had\ncalled Alderman Rowley to the\n\n=== 43_1868-11-27_pna_sn84024738_00271741959_1868112701_0524.json\nThe daily dispatch. [volume] (Richmond [Va.]) 1850-1884 · 1868-11-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n\nLeaC@Dark working common to good, ssasA;\nane a wrapping. Sl4 soGs7. 50. sun-cursd CON- 1\nmon to medium, $40 sO6Sl4; good to fine, slsN go.\nYellow wrappers. common to medium, s29GsaS;\ngood to extra (fancy), s\"@ss5.\n\n\nRichmond Prices-Current.\n\n\nTHURSDAY, Novem