\nRichmond Cattle Market.\n| THURSDAY, November %, 1863.\nfair 11EGilp\n\n=== 3_1920-12-05_p90_sn83045774_00271744110_1920120501_0186.json\nThe New York herald. · 1920-12-05\ncrops: None\nheadline: Like Novel, Like Review\n---\nl appreciate My responsibility in\nrecommending Steve's new book. Win\nsome Winnie\" for My wife's aunt al\nways reads My reviews to find out\nwhat not to get at the public library.\nBut ! gladly lend My name Con re-\nquesD to Steve's stuff, because any\nbody who can make banquet audi\nence laugh till midnight in prohibition\ntimes deserves all the support he can\nget\n\n\nNoTE.-7Next we have\n\n\nTHE IMPRESSIVE REVIEW\n\n\nFiction has been so standardized that\nthe picturesque heterogeneousness Of\nhalf century ago has been sadly di-\nminished. Against this abominable\nmodernized Standardization arises that\npast master of satire and wit-Prof.\nstephen Leacock His most recent\nwork. \"Winsome Winnie and Other\nNonsense Novels\" comes to us guised\nas humor, but even the undiscerning\nwill detect that he has struck straight\nat the vitals of modern literature in\nall it's horrid decadence, using as\nweapons his sharp sword of ridicule\nand the blasting power of his un-\nmatched ability to score at the ex-\npense of his contemporaries without\nstraining for effect.\n\n\nNorE.-It betokens vast knowl-\n\n\nedge on the part of a reviewer to\n\n\nprove that some one who wrote\n\n\nbook never wrote it at all. Recall\n\n\nIng the Shakespeare-Bacon contro-\n\n\nversy and others we now put the\n\n\nCorona on the baby's lap and write\nTHE WISE GUY REVIEW:\n\n\nWhile Stephen Leacock's name is\nattached to the new volume of satirical\nnovelettes under the title Of Win\nsome Winnie\" it has come to us that\nthe real author of the work is none\nother than Mark Twain. This belief in\nliterary circles has gained credence\never since 1t became known that Prof.\nLeacock was seen experimenting with\na ouija board at a Toronto tea\n\n\n1t has been pointed out that Lea\ncock is not the kind of a man who\nwould waste time reading seasons\ncrop of love stories, civil war tales. de-\ntective yarns, political novels, narra-\ntives of the sea and other products of\nleading literary lights. And if he never\nread them it would manifestly be i'm\npossible for him to have outranked the\nrankest popular sellers.\n\n\nOn the other hand. Mark Twain has\nhad virtually nothing to do since his\ndeath. The savants, consequently, hold\nthe theory that Mark Twain did the\nwork, while Prof. Leacock with the\naid OF d ouija board gets the credit.\nA very simple premise. is it not? Yes,\nvery simple.\n\n\nNoTE.-Our wife, who is serious\n\n\nminded, has read over our shoulder\n\n\nto here. Now she suggests that we\n\n\ntry to write\n\n\nA HUMOROUS REVIEW.\n\n\nWe can't!\n\n=== 26_1843-07-14_pna_sn84023127_00200290197_1843071401_0541.json\nBurlington free press. [volume] (Burlington, Vt.) 1827-1865 · 1843-07-14\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nremember, and remember only, the lost hus-\nband of my spirit-world. perform, coldly\nand scornfully, the unheavenly necessities of\nthis temporary existence and from the WIN\ndows of my prison (black-like the glimpses\nof the midnight heaven they let in) look out\nfor the coming Of my spirit-lord. Lonely !!\nlonely\n\n\nThou wouldst know, perhaps, what SEM\nblance l bear since my mortal separation\nfrom thee. Alas the rose, not the lily,\nreIgns upon my cheek would not dis\nappoint thee though of tina, there is little\nfear, for thou lowest for the spirit only. But\nbelieve not, because health holds me rudely\ndown, and seem not fragile and ready to\ndepart; believe not, oh bridegroom of my\nsoul that bear willingly my neshy fetter,\nor endure with patience the degrading hom-\nage to its beauty. For there are soulless\nworms who think me fair. Ay,i the strength\nand freshness of my corporal covering,\nthere are those who rejoice Oh MCKEE\nry mockery\n\n\nList to me, 1tllurel-(for must have a\nname to call thee by, and, till thou brea,hcst\nthy own scraphlc name into my ear, be thou\nI,huriel y List! I would meet the in the\ndarkness only Thou shall not see me with\nthy mortal eyes Penetrate the past, and\nremember the smoke curl of wavy lightness\nin which noated to thy embrace! Re-\nmember the sunset cloud TO which we retir-\ned the starry lamps that hung over our\nslumbcrs And on the soltest whisper of\nour voices let thy thoughts pass to mine\nSpeak not aloud ! Murmur murmur MUIR\nmuir\n\n\n'Dost thou know. Tthuriel, would fain\nprove to thee my freedom from the tram.\nmels of this world! In what chance shape\nthy accident of clay must be cast, | know\nnot. Ay, and | care not | would thou\nvert humpback, Ithuriel would thou\nvert disguised as monster, my spirit hus-\nband SO would | prove TO thee my eleva.\nton above mortality So would show thee\nthat in the range Of eternity for which we are\nwelded moments covering darkens thee\nnot; that, like star sailing through a cloud\nthy brightness is remembered while it i.\neclipsed; that thy lone would recognize thy\nvoice, be aware Of thy presence, adore thee\nas she was celestially wont; ay, thougl\nthou vert imprisoned in the likeness of {;\n: reptile lone care for mortal beauty Ha\n!, ha ha ha-IIa ha ha\n\n\nCome to me, lthuriel My heart writhe\nin its cell for converse with thee am sick\n: thoughted! My spirit wings its thin finger\nto play with thy ETHEREAL hair My ear,hl.\ncheek, though it obstinately refuses to pale\n, tingles with fever for thy coming. Glide\nto me in the shadow of eve-soitly ! softly\n, Address p at the NJ office.\nThine, lone s.\n\n=== 1_1908-10-17_pna_sn88086023_00202194631_1908101701_1321.json\nEast Oregonian : E.O. (Pendleton, OR) 1888-current · 1908-10-17\ncrops: None\nheadline: SPOOK GAVE BELIEVER\n\n\nWRONG KIND OF ADVICE\n\nASSERTS Her Guide Advised Marriage.\nShe WCds and Now Seeks Divorce.\n---\nDenver. oct. ia divorce COM\nplant has been filed against Dewitt\n10. Rima, by Mrs Cornella RIma,\nwho, although an ordained minister OF\nthe Suirituallst rhurch. seems % have\nbeen given the wrong steer by spooks\nwhen she asked their advice about\nmarrying\n\n\nHe refused to support me. ana\nI'VE grown tired Of supporting him\nIS the way Mrs Rlma put 3, when\nseen mt her home. i2l South Lincoln\navenue.\n\n\nput what ] didn't understand\nabout the thing Is how my guide\"\never let me marry him have given\nreligious counsel 20 hundreds of low\ncrs and their marriages We're always\nhappy: but I sure was handed %\nlemon.\n\n\n\"Thc spirits told me ne was q good\nman. and to marry him; but first\nmake him give up the saloon busi-\nness. He ala this all right. but moth\ning was said about his doing anything\nelse. and he hasn't done a lick Of\nwork since. but just laid around and\nlet me support him\"\n\n=== 71_1920-03-27_p10_sn83045433_00280764085_1920032701_0446.json\nThe Washington herald. · 1920-03-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: First spiritualist Church.\n---\n'or the Threshold of the Unseen\"\nwill be the subject of a talk by the\nRev. Alfred H. Terry. pastor of the\nFirst Spiritualist Church, at the\nIythian Temple tomorrow evening.\nSpirit messages will follow the ad-\ndress.\n\n=== 20_1879-04-11_pna_sn83032041_00212477710_1879041101_0201.json\nThe Cambria freeman. [volume] (Ebensburg, Pa.) 1867-1938 · 1879-04-11\ncrops: None\nheadline: \"THE HAUNTED HOUSE,\n\n4 Strange hut True story Related by\n\n\nDan J. Garrity i'm Boston Pilot.\n---\nThe time OF my narrative dates back\nto the year 1870. The events occurred\nin the city of Springfield. Ohio.\n\n\nSituated in the very centre of the city\nwas a mansion. old and lonely in aspect.\nthe property of a Mr.. FoOs. 1t had been\nfor several years Uninhabited. when\nstrange reports began to circulate Per\nsons who passed there at midnight, and\nother uncanny hours, often saw strange\nlights and heard stranger sounds, until\npeople believed that it was haunted.\n\n\nAt length two young men of the town\nresolved to see whether it was haunted\nor not, and thus put the fears and gOS-\nsipings of the towns people to an end.\n\n\nAccordingly they took up their quar-\ntiers at the mansion. About eight\nO'Clock they lit their candles, and made\nthemselves comfortable to receive their\nghoStly visitors.\n\n\nThe clock struck nine, ten, eleven. and\nit was now nearly twelve O'Clock. the\ntime for ghosts to appear. The great\nclock in st. Raphael's church began to\nstrike one. two, three. four, five, six.\nseven, eight. nine, ten, eleven, t,reIre.\nand then-No sooner had the sOnorous\npeals died away. than the candles were\nsuddenly OXtnguiShed. and they were\nleft in darkness. After a few minutes\nbright. circular light became visible\nnear one of the walls, and what terrible\nspectacle was brought to view \\ man\nwas stretched from tree with a rope\naround his neck. while a box lay at his\nfeet within reach-he was a suicide\n/ His distorted features and glaring eyes\nwere plainly visible, while his tongue.\nblack and swollen. hung at least half a\nfoot from his month To complete the\nhorrible picture. hundreds of demons\ntod\nThen followed wild burst of laughter\nand the apparition vanished.\nThe young men had not expected this.\nWhile this strange scene was enactmg\nthey sat stUpefied with fear, their hair\nactually standing on end.\n\n\nAt length their horrible spell was\nbroken, and without even stopping to\nsnatch their hats, they fled the house\nAs they were fleeing along the street.\nthey ran right into the arms of tall,\nmuScUlaTman, who seeing their alright\ned condition. frightened his grasp upon\nthem, and asked them to explain their\nfright.\n\n\nThey told him all.\n\n\nAfter they had finished their story,\nthe detective for it was no other than\nDetective John Burnside. of New \\ Ork.\nwho was visiting friends there told\nthem Ito say nothing about it, but wait\nand hope and he would discover the\nmystery if possible\n\n\nAbout two weeks after the detective\nreturned hOme-so his friends thought.\n\n\nIn course of time the young men dis\ncovered that they conld no longer keep\nthe secret. and 50 they divulged it.\n\n\n1t raised great excitement, of course,\nand the people Of the city gave the place\na wide berth.\n\n\nThe strange sounds and sights We're\ncontinued. until the people believed\nwithout a doubt that the house was\nhaunted.\n\n\nWe Will pass over year.\n\n\nOne morning a posse of police, headed\nby Il tall man in plain clothes, approach\ned the FuOs mansion. unlocked the door,\nand passed in.\n\n\nIn a few minutes the sound of pistol\nShots was heard. and then all was still.\nIn few minutes the posse emerged from\nthe house. looking much larger. What\ncould il mean\n\n\nAnd now, little Ants. my narrative is\nat an end, and Detective Burnside will\nfinish the story.\n\n\nNARRATIVE by DETECTIVE BURN\n\n\nSIDE.\n\n\nAfter | left the two young men, said\nDetective Burnside afterwards in telling\nthe story. my mind was made up\nwould discover the mystery.\n\n\nAccordingly. the next evening. | pro-\nceeded to the mansion. It was a mere\nrepetition OF the story of the young men,\nas far as the apparitiOn. As soon as the\nclock struck twelve my candle was\nblown ont by gust Of wind | know.\ncaused by the slamming of doors. And\nwhat a terrible sight met my eye. |\nshudder now when | think of it, 50 ter\nribly real it looked.\n\n\nThere in the same spot that the young\nmen had described | saw young and\nbeautiful girl on her knees \\ form\nwas bending over her. its hand clas ped\nher long golden hair, while a keen.\nbloody knife was drawn across her fair\nwhite throat, while torrents of crimson\nblood dripped down upon the floor.\n\n\n| regained by self-posseSSion enough tC\ndraw my pistol. aim. and fire. y wild\nlaugh followed. and my bullet was heard\ncrashing through the wainscoting.\nThe apparition vanished slowly. anC\nhappening to turn around discovered\nthe mystery.\n\n\n1t was gang of some sort. / wa:\nsure, and | was resolved to capture\nthem. A few weeks after | joined tn,\ngang. 10y means known only to myself\na professional secret. There were coun\n, terfeiters, burglars. and everything els'\n, among them. I Will not weary the read\n\n\neT with a description of the numerOu\n\n\nburglaTieS they committed. or of the va\n\n\nrious WAYS in which they manufacture,\n= and - showed their queer money\ne After staying with them for about\nt year, | resolved to break up the gang\n2 for l had now ample evidence to convic\nD them.\n\n\ne Accordingly. l obtained the posse q\n+ police. and raided the house. After\ne slight resistance they were captured.\nI When they found out who their cap\n2 tor was they threatened me with ven\nI geance dire, if they ever escaped bu\n+ they never did, for there was not one c\n' them but had committed enough crime\n+ TO condemn them for life.\nIS \\ few weeks after the gang We're sen\n4 to prison ! took opportunity to sho'\nG some visitors the house, and explain tn\nq secret of the ghosts In ther room whel\nV the apparitiOns appeared | showed then\ne a secret door. and behind it was an a\nq cove. This was the place where IL\nII robbers had hidden themselves ever\ne night, and. when the curious came\nl. see the ghosts. they frightened thel\ne away by means of a magic lantern. NJ\nd tale is done.\n\n\nle DANIEL SCHEIRER. shoemaker.\ne Richmond township, Berks county HI\nle in his possession stone which he us\nd i'm his shop. which was found by h\nn - 1. II.\n\n=== 8_1922-09-07_pna_sn84026824_0027176795A_1922090701_1098.json\nShepherdstown register. [volume] (Shepherdstown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1849-1955 · 1922-09-07\ncrops: None\nheadline: The Phantom Fleet Of the Arctic.\n---\nne fIdnlOn rect Oi the Afctic-\n\n\nEvery winter deserted ice-locked\nships are seen in the mists of the\npolar seas, drifting at the mercy OF\nwind and current, their broken masts\nand crumbling rigging hung with\nghostlke icicles, and their decks and\ncabins buried in drifts of snow. AT\nleast, so runs the stories of Eskimo\nnarrators.\n\n\nA Russian side-wheeler, the \"Polo-\ntofski,\" built 20 years ago, is an ad-\ndition TO this phantom fleet\" She\nwas discovered, toward evening, by a\nparty of Eskimos hunting walrus.\nPlans were made to board her next\nmorning, but the during the night the\nchanging wind swept the far north\ninto the UNEMPLOYED wastes.\n\n\nThe \"Polotofski,\" according TO the\nonly accounts available, was caught IN\nthe ice at ST. Michael, in December,\n1915, and disappeared the following\nspring in the great storm off Cape\nNome. 1t was thought that she had\nbeen ground to kindling and sunk.\n/ Vessels have been disappearing in\nthis great unknown ever since the be-\nginning of arctic navigation In\nlS45, the \"Erebus\" and the \"Terror\".\nOf the British navy, sailed with l29\nsouls on board to attempt the nCrth-\nwest passage. They we're last seen\nby civilized men in Baffin Bay. i.iore\nthan a score of ships, with crews &C\nnearly 1000 men, at an expense of\nmillions of dollars, vainly sought, be-\ntween lS47 and 1888, news of the\nmissing squadron. The fate of the\nvessels is shrouded in myStery\n\n\nAnother mystery is that of the\npresident\" She was due in Liver\npool from New York in November.\n1871. Severe weather had prevailed\nin the Atlantic and unusual quantities\nof ice were reported in low attitudes\nFor months the owners queriei ada\ncent ports in vain effort to get some\nnews of her. Years later there was\nfound in the loebook of a Portugal\nsailing vessel an entry which stated\ntht a large steamer, resembling the\npresident\" with her machinery ap-\nparently disabled, had been sighted.\nThis entry was dated a month after the\ndisappearance of the \"President\"\n\n\nCould these ships have drifted\nnorthward? Are they, too, part of that\nfleet of silent ships held hard and fast\nin the great ice pack, white with frost\nand manned by frozen crews?\n\n\nThe little schooner, Teddy Bear\"\nmissing eight months and believed lost,\nwas found this June frozen in, near\nthe mouth of the Potem River, II miles\nsouth of Emma, a village on East Cape.\n/ Siberia.\n\n\nOnly miraculous luck saved the Ted\n/ dy Bear\" from joining the phantom\nfleet, of which the \"Polotofski\" re-\nmains the sole naturalization.\n\n=== 32_1879-01-20_p1_sn85042331_0021247082A_1879012001_0123.json\nBismarck tribune. [volume] (Bismarck, D.T. [N.D.]) 1878-1884 · 1879-01-20\ncrops: None\nheadline: A GAY TIME WITH COLLINS.\n\nBlSMA RCK'S MEDIUM ROLLICK.\nING WITH THE SPIRITS.\n\nwonderful Revelations from Beyond\n\n\nthe srave---e:raduhl Development\nOf Madame La kecher---The Story\n\n\nOf Tut Up Job AuthOritively Do-\nnied---Public Seances Discontinued,\n---\nTHE SPOOkS.\n\n\nSince the earlier revelations by The\nTRIBUNE of the remarkable spiritual man\nifestations under the auspices of Col.\nSweet and Madame La Secher, some of the\nmost respectable people of Bismarck have\ntaken an interest IN the seances, and as the\nmedium develops under the Colonel's\nmanipulations, the nature OF her COM\nmunications grows more and more start\nling. Col. Sweet is thoroughly in earnest\nin his work, and in order 10 bring him\nself physically more en- rapport with the\nspirit land, has taken to low diet and per-\nfeet care Of himself\" Madam La Secher\nhas apparently passed through the sphere\nof the evil spirits and has acquired high\nsocial standing among the blessed, with\nwhom she r#\nGAMBOLs IN HER FRANCES,\n\n\nand from whom she receives messages Tar\nmore ooheren, and satisfactory than any\nthat have ever characterized the ordinary\ncircle\n\n\nAt a recent sitting prominent clergy\nman Of Bismarck asked some questions\nconcerning Il matter OF which no one but\nhimself and his wife were cognizant, and\nwhen the medium related what he had\ndone, the reverend gentleman was dumb\nfounded, and his good wife almost tainted.\nWhile in a clairvoyant state, the medium\nhas impaired some extremely valuable IN\nformation to several gentlemen. and alta\nseance last night she shocked two citizens\nbv private communications which they\nREFUSE TO REVEAL.\n\n\nbut which they say are startling in their\nfidelity to facts already in their posses\nsian.\n\n\nIt has been stated by some gossips that\nthis whole spiritualistic racket is a set up\nJob, born of spirit of deviltry and orig\nmated and carried out by the editor OF\nThe TRIBUNE. a reporter for this paper\ninterrogated Mr.. Huntley on the subject,\nand catechised him severely, but MI\nHuntley denied all knowlssge Of tue; g st:\nfair, and disclaimed any fh!ersst in it.\nfurther than the natural curiosity which\nevery man has when confronted with the\nmysterious. He had attended few OF the\nsittings III his journalistic capacity, ana\nnot otherwise, and ne concludea WIN lhe\nremark that when he had time to go fool.\nIng ArOunu Will Spli AVo, 'ASJ sSOoA>< ~ W\nthat character that exhilierate rather than\ndepress, and of whose origin and genuine\nness the label would be sufficient guar:\nantec.\n\n\nThe Colonel has erected\n\n\nA CAB1NET\n\n\nand expects that Madame will soon be\nable to materialize the friends who have\ncmployed her as medium of communica-\nton with this world. Beyond the changing\nof the letters and the discolDration OF the\nwater spoken of in last weeks issue, there\nhave been no physical manifestations, but\nMadame has been rapidly developing up\nto the peint when she will be able to pro-\nduce in person those with whom she now\nconverScs.\n\n\nCol Sweet has concluded 70 discontinue\nthe public receptions for while, and con-\nfine his efforts to private seances, or \"de.\nveloping circles,' as they are called, until\nhe can present Madame in less nervous\nstate and insure the success of all her un-\ndertakings.\n\n=== 12_1896-11-01_p1_sn83030272_00175042908_1896110101_0014.json\nThe sun. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1833-1916 · 1896-11-01\ncrops: None\nheadline: VAR ON SOCIAL TICE.\n\nDisreputabie Women In London Driven\n\n\nfrom Ono Haunt to Another.\n---\nLONDON. Oct. 31.-The disreputable woman In\nLondon is being haunted out OF her accustomed\nhaunts to congregate in others. Several attempts\nhave been made by tho local authorities to deal\nwith this evil. The latest originated with the\nPlccadllly vestry. which summoned a general\nconference of London ~vestries. which was held\non Oct. Ia. 1t was agreed that all the London\nlocal authorities take concerted action for the\ntotal suppression OF the systematic vice which\ninfested the streets.\n\n\nThis concerted actor\" has since been In\noperation. with the result that tho local authori-\nties. after a weeks trial, have in certain dis\ntricts ordered tho police to resort to former\nmethods of dealing with disorderly people. The\nfooltnG around OF purity vigilance societies has\nhas been found especially obnoxious to the\npolice management Of the districts.\n","numLines":1889,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1889}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bb37bec1-c6ab-4521-a21e-3931282a3d2a","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
