one has\n809\tchanged materially. 1t can no longer be said that\n810\tmembership in the Union Club is equivalent to ad-\n811\tmission TO all Of the best houses. Even its strongest\n812\t\n813\t\n814\tand oldest members at least of them, feel that\n815\tn does not meet the social requirements as 1t dld\n816\tformerly.\n817\t\n818\t\n819\tMen who have retired from business seem anxious\n820\tn escape from business cares, at second hand\n821\tand are more eager for social relaxation and diversion\n822\tthan for a mere resort at which to annihilate time\n823\tsuch men are not satisfied with surroundings and as\n824\tsocations which remind them Of their former business\n825\tresponsibilities where the conversations Is all ABOUT\n826\tbusiness affairs and where the diversions are III\n827\tterrupted by the constant absorption OF a large enc\n828\tment in affairs OF the day. They are men VINO have\n829\tearned the right diversions, and their club Is the\n830\tmedium through which they expect to obtain them\n831\tThen there are many Of high social position who\n832\tare still in business bat who wish to take an actNe\n833\tmart in society for the sake of their iamcs OF lor INC\n834\tMeasure they themselves enjoy. such men generally\n835\tlike TO leave business cares behind them when they\n836\tleave their offices for the day. At their club they\n837\tlook for diversion, and it is not pleasant to ma thec\n838\tANYTHING that will remind them OF the Office To say\n839\tu MAINLY there is not much of anything but shop\n840\ttalk at the Union Club any more. II Is purely ana\n841\tsimply Wall street club, and in the ios Oh ii'aioc\n842\twhich has envelOped it the social functions \" inc OF\n843\tganization have been lost.\n844\t\n845\t\n846\tAlmost identical observations could be made con\n847\tcorning the other large clubs, all of which, no doubt\n848\thave strong social element distnctively their own\n849\tbut which are sustained chiefly, rather enveloped\n850\tby principles dis