rter. [volume] (Grand Rapids [i.e. Wisconsin Rapids], Wis.) 1857-1923 · 1884-07-03\n1190\tcrops: None\n1191\theadline: a Methodist Ghost Story.\n1192\t---\n1193\tOne secret Of the power which at\n1194\ttends the simple ministrations of the\n1195\tMethodist preachers of the primitive\n1196\ttype was that spiritual and unseen\n1197\tthings were to them what they are tru-\n1198\tII, more real than the objects of mere\n1199\tsense. The training of some of these\n1200\tmen was favorable to this An illus\n1201\ttratiOn was once given by an old man\n1202\twith whom l used to chat in the neigh\n1203\tbOrhoCd of BOscastIe. One evening,\n1204\tas we sat by the lireside, | referred to\n1205\tthe old Minister Church not far of, in q\n1206\tromantic valley, and spoke of the curl\n1207\tGus, CHASTITY legends that foated about\n1208\tit. Yes, SIR\" said he, ''queer things\n1209\thave been said, and queer things have\n1210\tbeen met with, say what we may.\n1211\tThere was one John Warden, sir-yes,\n1212\tJohn Warden that afterwards got to be\n1213\t4 preacher. John Warden, sir, was q\n1214\tfarmers boy-a labOrer, poor fellow.\n1215\tThe Lord touched his heart, sir. and\n1216\tJohn Warden turned Methodist Well,\n1217\tsir, his master and all about him turn\n1218\ted upon him, and the poor boy had to\n1219\tbear all sorts of persecution. One\n1220\tnight, rs they were all sitting around\n1221\tthe fire in the kitchen, and many ways\n1222\thad been tried to put poor John out of\n1223\ttemper, the master said ut last: 'Relie-\n1224\tion, ehn said he. What kind of relio-\n1225\tion hast thee got, John, Ell? Why,\n1226\tIncest be afraid to 90 down to the\n1227\tchurch tonight at - o clock Religion,\n1228\teh! This was a sort of challenge,\n1229\tsir, to try whether John was good\n1230\tenough TO be above fear Poor John,\n1231\tsir, in the simplicity of his heart,\n1232\tthought the credit Of his religion was\n1233\treally ut stake, and screwing up his\n1234\tcourage, he said quietly: No, [ bean't\n1235\tafraid to go to the church by night or\n1236\tby day WIN thee go tonight, then,\n1237\tand let's see what your religion is\n1238\tmade OPP Yes, | will They waited\n1239\ttill midnight, and John started, sir.\n1240\tThe farm was not far up from the val\n1241\tley. The question was now they should\n1242\tknow he had been TO the church. A\n1243\tplan was hit upon. He was to take\n1244\tlarge spike nail and u hammer and to\n1245\tdrive the nail into the church door.\n1246\tJohn went off with his hammer, nail,\n1247\tand \\ lantern; and he told me after\n1248\tward, years afterward when he was a\n1249\tpreacher-yes, sir, he never forgot it\n1250\t\n1251\t\n1252\tAS L WCHG gov IL iluOJI1 Tic trees, says\n1253\tJohn, and the wind moaned, [ felt\n1254\tlittle queer, but [ got to the church all\n1255\tright, though just as [ was going into\n1256\tthe church the wind came whistling\n1257\tround the church, and out went my\n1258\tlight. declare l then wished myself\n1259\taway from the place. 1t was pitchy\n1260\tdark. [ asked God TO help me. and /\n1261\tthen felt my way to the door, put my\n1262\tnail to it and gave the first blow. [ felt\n1263\tq tingling all over me as the echoes\n1264\twent round inside the church. They\n1265\tsounded hollow. But l picked up cour-\n1266\tage and hammered away until the nail\n1267\twas long way in. Then turned and\n1268\tCROPPED my way back to the farm.\n1269\tThey laughed at me. and said they\n1270\twould go down in the morning to be\n1271\tsure | had been there. They went\n1272\tdown early, and there was the nail.\n1273\tBut when they opened the door to see\n1274\twhether it had went through, oh, how\n1275\tthey stared at one another! There was\n1276\tthe nail turned back, turned in and\n1277\tcIenched to the door as if the cleverest\n1278\tcarpenter in the world had done it. F\"\n1279\tSunday Magazine.\n1280\t\n1281\t=== 5_1845-06-18_p1_sn85026593_00202198065_1845061801_0201.json\n1282\tYpsilanti sentinel. [volume] (Yps